      "paperId": "6e37c91d36e310acdd637490b2d5bb888c897ba3",
      "title": "The \u201cHic Homo Intelligit\u201d Controversy: Averroes versus Aquinas. A Review of: Ogden, S. Averroes on Intellect. From Aristotelian Origins to Aquinas\u2019s Critique. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2022, 281 pp.",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Kirill V. Karpov",
          "authorId": "2353522797"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Stephen Ogden\u2019s monograph, Averroes on Intellect: From Aristotelian Origins to Aquinas\u2019s Critique, constitutes a foundational study devoted to one of the most radical and contentious doctrines in the history of philosophy \u2014 the theory of the single and separate intellect, developed by the classical Islamic thinker Ibn Ru\u0161d (Averroes). Professor Ogden, a recognized scholar in medieval Islamic and Western philosophy, undertakes not merely a historical-philosophical survey but offers a profound systematic analysis, striving to rehabilitate Averroes\u2019s conception and to demonstrate its philosophical coherence and relevance for contemporary debates. The key thesis of Averroes, which Ogden subjects to meticulous scrutiny, posits that the material (or potential) intellect, responsible for the cognition of universals, is single for all humankind, immaterial, separate from bodies, and eternal. This proposition, which drew fierce criticism from Thomas Aquinas, is treated by the author not as an eccentric misconception but as a robust and internally consistent philosophical system. Ogden provides a detailed analysis of Averroes\u2019s two principal arguments in defense of his doctrine \u2014 the \u201cDeterminate Particular Argument\u201d and the \u201cUnity Argument\u201d \u2014 demonstrating their logical structure and revealing their respective strengths and weaknesses. The central and most provocative element of Ogden\u2019s investigation is the interpretation he proposes, which he designates as the \u201cerror theory\u201d. Within the framework of this interpretation, the author provides an original response to Thomas Aquinas\u2019s primary objection, hic homo intelligit. Ogden compellingly argues that, according to the strict metaphysical teaching of Averroes, the individual human being is not, in the literal sense, the subject of the act of thinking; this act belongs to the single, impersonal intellect. However, humans initiate the process of cognition through their volition and become aware of its results via a connection to their unique sensory images (phantasms), which generates in us the illusion of individual thinking. In the final part of the review, I will draw several parallels between Averroes\u2019s doctrine and approaches within contemporary philosophy of mind.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.21146/2949-1126-2025-3-2-103-117",
